generatedCommentsNodes.comment:
The `processDoubles`, `processInts`, and `processLongs` methods contain duplicate logic for handling sorted and unsorted blocks. This can be refactored to reduce redundancy.
```java
private static void processValues(DoubleBlock.Builder builder, int position, DoubleBlock valuesBlock, double percentile) {
    // Common logic here
}
private static void processValues(IntBlock.Builder builder, int position, IntBlock valuesBlock, double percentile) {
    // Common logic here
}
private static void processValues(LongBlock.Builder builder, int position, LongBlock valuesBlock, double percentile) {
    // Common logic here
}
```
The `calculateLongPercentile` method uses `BigDecimal` for calculations when the values are outside the safe range of representable double integers. This is correct but can be optimized by checking if both values are within the safe range before creating `BigDecimal` instances.
```java
if (upperValue < MAX_SAFE_LONG_DOUBLE && lowerValue > -MAX_SAFE_LONG_DOUBLE) {
    long difference = upperValue - lowerValue;
    return lowerValue + (long) (fraction * difference);
}
```
The `calculateDoublePercentile` method uses a different approach when the values are separated by zero. This is correct but can be simplified by using a single formula that handles all cases.
```java
double difference = upperValue - lowerValue;
return lowerValue + fraction * difference;
```

